the hamburg-based group rainforest  rescue is being sued for protesting  against the widespread destruction of  rainforests by the indonesian group  corindo  since 2001 corindo is thought to have  cleared a total of 57 000 hectares of  rainforest in indonesia  that's an area the size of chicago or  seoul  the company rejects accusations that it  systematically cleared forest and  illegally set fires  but not everyone believes that  together with 10 other environmental  organizations rainforest rescue cooled  on german industrial giant siemens and  wind turbine maker nordex to reconsider  their collaboration with corindo in  2016.  almost three years later the tiny german  environmental group was sued for  slandering corindo  rainforest rescue's main concern was  that the lawsuit was aimed not only at  them but at silencing their indonesian  partners too  they are constantly being intimidated by  corindo  it's a company that likes to demonstrate  its far-reaching influence  they're basically saying we can drag  your little partner in germany to court  from indonesia  and you're just small fry  what are you going to do about it  but alarm bells began ringing in hamburg  was this less a case of slander and more  to do with intimidation  often the costs of supplying evidence  for their defense leave small ngos  journalists and bloggers on the brink of  financial ruin  of course it's incredibly difficult  especially when you're trying to get  witnesses from on the ground  we've experienced a huge amount of fear  among those locals who could be  considered as witnesses  if the rainforest rescue group weren't  backed up by evidence from the big  international ngos such as greenpeace  who've published reports and aerial  shots including satellite images  then hands down they'd lose this lawsuit  lawyers for the corindo group responded  to dw's inquiries on the current lawsuit  by saying that it is not up to the court  and the parties involved to decide on  the justification of the claim  in europe there's been a general trend  towards people being brought to court  for publicly raising ecological and  social issues  and that trend has been given its own  name slap  that's an acronym for strategic lawsuit  against public participation  critics say the aim of these lawsuits is  pure intimidation  while the plaintiff has little to gain  journalists and campaigners are simply  worn down through harassment  in 2017 maltese journalist daphne  caruana galicia was killed she'd been  investigating government corruption  reporters without borders say at the  time of her death she was the subject of  40 slap lawsuits  environmental activist carl bear is also  being sued  he'd been campaigning against the use of  pesticides in apple plantations in south  tyrol italy  formerly an employee at the munich  institute for the environment today carl  bear is a member of the german  parliament he's currently facing  defamation charges in a bolsano court  in 2017  baer launched a campaign against the  massive use of toxic pesticides in south  tyrol's apple mono culture  the regional council as well as 1400  apple farmers filed criminal complaints  against him  we initially thought these claims were  part of an election campaign stunt we  didn't think we'd hear any more about  them  then actual charges were brought by the  state prosecutor and there was a  two-year investigation but really it's  an abuse of the legal system because  obviously it was a political issue being  transferred to the legal sphere now it's  being negotiated in court when it really  belongs on the public in parliamentary  scrutiny  carl bear and the environment institute  say they have evidence of every single  pesticide  it's cost a huge amount of time and  money  as well as the costs of travel and  translations bear is living with a  psychological pressure felt by many  others facing similar intimidation  lawsuits  but he says there are many who have it  harder than him  other people such as bloggers who work  unpaid in environmental protection or  anti-corruption are often much worse off  as far as resources are concerned they  end up mentally and financially worn out  by cases like this  intimidation lawsuits like this have yet  another effect anyone with reason to  fear being taken to court will often end  up staying silent or censoring  themselves  it sends a signal to everyone else in my  case that was south chiron  basically they're saying if you stick  your neck out you could end up getting  your head knocked off  the eu commission and the german  government are now trying to stop such  slap lawsuits  it's surprisingly a serious problem  right on the rise we have increased  numbers  especially of cross-border cases so you  as a journalist can be sued in another  member state so it means you have to  hire the lawyers there and to to defend  yourself which is costly which is time  demanding  so the problem is on the rise and uh  we  see that uh  it's part of the political and economic  pressure on media  yes so that's why we decided to  design some legislation but also some  non-legislative actions  vera gerova knows it'll be hard for all  eu members to agree on what defines a  slap lawsuit  some countries after all like to use  such legal measures against undesirable  critics  so when is something slander  when is it justified criticism  it's important that companies must still  be able to take action if false reports  or allegations are made against them  but there are times when this right to  legal recourse is abused as it always  has been and today we're seeing it  happen much more often  so we need a legal framework to end such  processes quickly  so small organizations don't suffer  [Music]  carl bear is glad the issue has finally  been picked up by policy policymakers  but he still faces several years before  his case comes to an end  [Music]  you
 

